{"evidence_id": "evd_lit_cudagraph_launch_overhead", "source_type": "literature", "paper_ids": ["pap_flash_attention_3"], "claim_ids": ["clm_fa3_intra_kernel_scope"], "run_ids": [], "experiment_id": "", "hypothesis_ids": ["hyp_cudagraph_fa3_walltime"], "facts": {"reported": "FlashAttention-3 reports large attention-kernel speedups from asynchrony and low precision. It optimizes work INSIDE the attention op and does not eliminate the CPU-side kernel-launch/dispatch overhead paid per op across a full training step, which CUDA-graph capture (torch.compile max-autotune) targets by recording and replaying the step's kernel sequence."}, "analysis": {"method": "Read FA3's contribution scope and contrast it with what CUDA-graph capture removes; argue the launch-overhead axis is unaddressed and therefore a live throughput lever in a time-budgeted frame.", "code_ref": "train.py:1772-1782 (COMPILE_MODE knob)"}, "trust": {"design": "not_applicable", "replication": "not_applicable", "scope_match": "weak", "directness": "weak", "limitations": ["Supports only the premise (launch overhead is a separate axis), NOT the conclusion that cudagraphs net-help here; that is the empirical question the pilot answers.", "FA3 evidence is about the H200 attention kernel, not about the cudagraph/VRAM tradeoff in this specific 5-min frame."]}, "artifact_paths": [], "created_at": "2026-07-25T09:00:00-07:00", "created_by": "claude-ledger", "supersedes_evidence_id": "", "assessment": {"relation": "supports", "strength": "weak", "limitations": ["Weakly supportive: establishes the axis exists, not its magnitude."]}, "tags": ["systems_kernel", "cudagraph", "throughput", "walltime_5min"], "fingerprint": "c363048a68e7ebc2"}
{"evidence_id": "evd_lit_ngram_sparse_grad_throughput", "source_type": "literature", "paper_ids": ["pap_recent_optim_galore"], "claim_ids": ["clm_galore_memory_lever"], "run_ids": [], "experiment_id": "", "hypothesis_ids": ["hyp_ngram_sparse_grad_walltime"], "facts": {"reported": "Memory-efficient training work (GaLore) establishes that cutting per-step gradient/optimizer memory footprint is a real lever. For n-gram VE tables specifically, train.py materializes and reduces a dense ~5.6 GB gradient per step at 64x; NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD replaces it with touched-row writes (~1.6 GB, constant in table size) and NGRAM_STATE_ROWWISE shrinks the optimizer second moment from (rows,dim) to (rows,1)."}, "analysis": {"method": "Contrast the baseline dense-gradient path with the sparse-grad + row-wise path documented in train.py:1784-1822 and 1661+; argue the reduced memory traffic can raise step throughput in a time-budgeted frame.", "code_ref": "train.py:1661-1822 (NGRAM_STATE_ROWWISE / NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD)"}, "trust": {"design": "not_applicable", "replication": "not_applicable", "scope_match": "weak", "directness": "weak", "limitations": ["Supports only the premise that gradient-memory footprint is reducible; whether that nets FASTER steps at 64x is the empirical question.", "The numerics are match-to-rounding, not bitwise identical, so a matched-step quality check is part of the pilot."]}, "artifact_paths": [], "created_at": "2026-07-25T09:40:00-07:00", "created_by": "claude-ledger", "supersedes_evidence_id": "", "assessment": {"relation": "supports", "strength": "weak", "limitations": ["Premise-level support only; magnitude unknown at 64x."]}, "tags": ["memory_retrieval", "ngram", "throughput", "walltime_5min"], "fingerprint": "f6adfbc59af52a92"}
{"evidence_id": "evd_lit_cautious_updates", "source_type": "literature", "paper_ids": ["pap_recent_optim_cautious"], "claim_ids": ["clm_recent_optim_cautious_1"], "run_ids": [], "experiment_id": "", "hypothesis_ids": ["hyp_cautious_update_walltime"], "facts": {"reported": "Cautious Optimizers zero out the components of a momentum-based update whose sign disagrees with the current gradient. The paper reports this improves training convergence/quality across Adam/Lion-family base optimizers as a one-line change, while preserving the base optimizer's Hamiltonian/Lyapunov descent guarantee. It is compute-negligible (an elementwise mask)."}, "analysis": {"method": "Assess whether extending the cautious mask (already on for Muon params) to the Adam-updated params improves matched-time val_bpb in this frame.", "code_ref": "train.py CAUTIOUS_UPDATE / CAUTIOUS_MUON"}, "trust": {"design": "adequate", "replication": "adequate", "scope_match": "weak", "directness": "adequate", "limitations": ["The paper's gains are on its own base optimizers/datasets, not this exact Muon+Adam recipe or the 300s frame; magnitude here is unknown.", "CAUTIOUS_MUON is already on, so part of the cautious benefit may already be captured; the marginal effect of adding it to Adam params could be small."]}, "artifact_paths": [], "created_at": "2026-07-25T11:00:00-07:00", "created_by": "claude-ledger", "supersedes_evidence_id": "", "assessment": {"relation": "supports", "strength": "moderate", "limitations": ["Direct support for the mechanism, but scope transfer to this frame is unverified."]}, "tags": ["optimizer", "cautious", "walltime_5min"], "fingerprint": "ae193691916b8173"}
{"evidence_id": "evd_lit_rho1_focal", "source_type": "literature", "paper_ids": ["pap_r2_data_rho1_selective_lm"], "claim_ids": ["clm_r2_data_rho1_selective_lm_1"], "run_ids": [], "experiment_id": "", "hypothesis_ids": ["hyp_rho1_focal_walltime"], "facts": {"reported": "Rho-1 (Selective Language Modeling) shows that concentrating the training loss on the most useful/high-signal tokens sharply improves data efficiency. train.py's RHO1_GAMMA is a self-referential focal variant (no reference model): it upweights tokens by w=ce**gamma, normalized to preserve loss scale. Costs one extra reduction='none' CE (negligible)."}, "analysis": {"method": "Assess whether focal upweighting of high-loss tokens improves per-step learning in the underfit 2000-step regime.", "code_ref": "train.py:1584-1607 (RHO1_GAMMA branch)"}, "trust": {"design": "adequate", "replication": "weak", "scope_match": "weak", "directness": "weak", "limitations": ["Rho-1 uses a REFERENCE MODEL to select tokens; RHO1_GAMMA is a self-referential focal reweight, which on an underfit LM risks upweighting genuinely-unlearnable/noisy tokens.", "Rho-1's gains are at larger scale/different setup; transfer to this 82M/2000-step frame is unverified."]}, "artifact_paths": [], "created_at": "2026-07-25T13:30:00-07:00", "created_by": "claude-ledger", "supersedes_evidence_id": "", "assessment": {"relation": "supports", "strength": "weak", "limitations": ["Supports the premise (token-selective loss helps efficiency), not the specific self-referential focal form."]}, "tags": ["data_order", "rho1", "focal_loss", "walltime_5min"], "fingerprint": "07a4a0b6dcfe2f4f"}
